@startuml pipeline
title The three stages — every step, with what goes in and what comes out

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #333333
skinparam ActivityBackgroundColor #FCFCFC

legend top
  Worked example, carried the whole way through:

  ""#[derive(SomeDerive)]""
  #[configuration(colour(ColourSetting::Red, Other(Blue)), name = "thing")]
  ""pub struct Thing;""

  |<#E8F4EA>| real and tested |
  |<#FFF4E5>| built, not wired |
  |<#FFD9D9>| todo!() |
  |<#EEEEEE>| aspirational |
endlegend

|#F5F5F5|EXTRACTION — walk and carry|

start

:**parse_macro_input!**
----
in   ""proc_macro::TokenStream""
out  ""syn::DeriveInput""; <<#E8F4EA>>

:**StructExtraction::extract_from**
----
in   ""&'ast DeriveInput""
out  ""Extracted<StructExtraction, &'ast DeriveInput>""
----
via  ""validate"" -> ""&'ast DataStruct""
then ""extract_each(data.fields.iter())""; <<#E8F4EA>>
note right
  ""#[from = source.data.fields]"" will
  generate this line. The ""Vec"" in the
  field's type picks ""extract_each"";
  the walk is never written out.
end note

:**FieldExtraction::extract_from**
----
in   ""&'ast syn::Field""
out  ""Extracted<FieldExtraction, &'ast Field>""
----
then ""extract_each(field.attrs.iter())""; <<#E8F4EA>>


:**SyntaxFieldAttributeExtraction::<Raw>::extract_from**
----
in   ""&'ast Attribute""   e.g. ""#[shape(AttributeKind::MetaList)]""
out  ""Extracted<SyntaxFieldAttributeExtraction<'ast, Raw>, &'ast Attribute>""
----
carries ""Unresolved<'ast, syn::Type>""
holding ""&'ast TokenStream"" = ""AttributeKind :: MetaList""; <<#FFF4E5>>
note right
  ""validate"" narrows to
  ""(&Attribute, SyntaxHelper)"" — the head is
  RESOLVED here, so no later stage repeats
  the comparison.

  The tokens are CARRIED, never read.
end note

:**.resolve()**  (optional, typestate transition)
----
in   ""SyntaxFieldAttributeExtraction<'ast, Raw>""  (consumed)
out  ""Extraction<SyntaxFieldAttributeExtraction<'ast, Parsed>>""
----
""Unresolved<Type>"" -> ""Resolved<Type>""
tokens kept either side; <<#FFF4E5>>
note right
  On the compiler-checked path this is
  NEVER called — the selector is spliced
  and rustc resolves it. Resolving stays
  available for a stage that must inspect.
end note

note right
  **TransformationExtraction is gone.**
  It used to sit beside this as a rival reader
  of the same ""&Attribute"". It was never a node:
  it held the author's transformation expression
  — a field path, a closure, a fn pointer —
  which is ""#[from]"" on the field that needs it.
  (#attribute/generic-grammar)
end note

|#FFF4E5|PROCESSING — understand|

:**Processor::process**
----
in   ""Self::Input"" = an extractor's ""Output"", whole
out  ""Extraction<Self::Output>""
----
children first, via ""process_each""; <<#E8F4EA>>
note right
  Handed over WHOLE — unwrapping would strip
  the source off exactly the value that needs
  it, since a failed extraction has no ""T"" to
  ask at all.

  **Optional stage.** ""#[derive(Processor)]""
  emits the identity, so downstream sees one
  shape and never an either-or.

  Bottom-up: ""process_each"" mirrors
  ""extract_each"", so a parent collects its
  children's OUTPUTS before combining.
end note

:**Opening::from(&Meta)**
----
in   ""&'ast syn::Meta""      ""configuration(..)""
out  ""Opening::List(ListBody(&'ast TokenStream))""; <<#E8F4EA>>
note right
  ""syn::Meta"" IS the attribute AST. Nothing
  invents a node layer beside it.
end note

:**ListBody::metas()**
----
in   ""ListBody<'ast>""       raw tokens
out  ""Punctuated<Meta, Token![,]>""
----
[ colour(..), name = "thing" ]; <<#E8F4EA>>
note right
  ""exprs()"" is the other reading, for bare
  literals — ""bounds(0, 64)"" is impossible
  without it, since a literal is not valid
  ""Meta"" at all.
end note

:**walk_keys(body, KEYS, accept)**
----
in   ""ListBody"" + ""&[&str]"" candidates
out  ""Result<()>"", errors COMBINED not aborted
----
owns UnknownKey / Duplicate / candidates; <<#E8F4EA>>

:**per field: ""<FieldTy as FromMeta>::from_meta""**
----
""colour"" : ""&Meta"" -> ""Vec<ColourSetting>""
""name""   : ""&Meta"" -> ""ConfigName(LitStr)""
----
uniform: the macro does not know the shape; <<#E8F4EA>>
note right
  ""Vec<T>"" is SHAPE-CHANGING — one ListBody in,
  many value positions out. ""Option<T>"" is
  shape-preserving. That asymmetry is where
  "arity lives in the type" is paid for.
end note

:**variants!: ""ColourSetting::try_from(&Meta)""**
----
""ColourSetting::Red"" -> ""ColourSetting::Red""    (unit, suffix-matched)
""Other(Blue)""        -> ""ColourSetting::Other(Ident)""  (tuple)
""Rgb(r = 12, ..)""    -> ""ColourSetting::Rgb { .. }""    (struct); <<#E8F4EA>>
note right
  THE place a value is matched by name,
  because the caller needs a VALUE and rustc
  can only check one, never hand one over.
  Cost: a renamed import is invisible.
  (#vocab/match-or-splice)
end note

:**assemble**
----
out  ""Configuration {""
     ""  colour: vec![Red, Other(Blue)],""
       name:   Some(ConfigName("thing")),
     ""}""
plus ""Vec<Reason>"" — value AND complaints; <<#EEEEEE>>

|#E8F4EA|GENERATION — hand it to rustc|

:**splice the selector verbatim**
----
in   ""Unresolved<Type>"" tokens, unread
out  ""type Selected = AttributeKind::MetaList;""
----
rustc resolves it, with the user's spans; <<#EEEEEE>>
note right
  Buys did-you-mean WITH a machine-applicable
  fix, and "but trait FromMeta<MetaList> is
  implemented for it" — neither writable by us.
end note

:**re-emit keys as a struct literal**
----
in   the key -> field mapping
out  ""Configuration { colur: .., name: .. }""
----
rustc gives UnknownKey / Missing / Duplicate; <<#EEEEEE>>
note right
  Three of the five ""ReasonKind"" variants,
  with did-you-mean, for free. ""WrongShape""
  and ""Ambiguous"" stay ours.
end note

:**Generator::generate**, then ""emit""
----
in   a processor's ""Output""
out  ""TokenStream"" — stub FIRST, then N ""compile_error!""
----
the stub always goes out; <<#E8F4EA>>
note right
  Without the stub, the missing impl cascades
  into "does not implement" at every use site
  and buries the real diagnostic. ""emit"" makes
  that mechanical rather than remembered.

  TODO(#typed-output/generate): this should
  return a TYPED syn item, not a raw stream.
  We BUILD this content and know its shape;
  handing it back untyped means nothing checks
  it until rustc parses it again — and it makes
  precise spans impossible.
  Carrier vs product: ""Unresolved"" and
  ""ListBody"" stay raw, deliberately.
end note

stop

@enduml
